---
import { readFileSync } from 'node:fs';
import Pod from '../../layouts/Pod.astro';

const HOST = 'mtg.subch.us';
const PORT = '17171';
const DISCORD = 'https://discord.gg/cGGjXK7Qa5';

// Artist credit comes from the fetch script's output so it cannot drift from
// the images actually on disk.
// Astro builds with cwd at the project root, so a plain relative path is
// steadier here than resolving against import.meta.url.
const credits = JSON.parse(readFileSync('public/mtg/img/credits.json', 'utf8')).credits;
// The five house tribes. They no longer get their own section - they are the
// site's colour identity, painted across the hero as a backdrop.
const TRIBES = [
  { key: 'elf', color: 'G' },
  { key: 'goblin', color: 'R' },
  { key: 'merfolk', color: 'U' },
  { key: 'angel', color: 'W' },
  { key: 'vampire', color: 'B' },
];
---

<Pod title="Goonmander">
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="tribe-strip" aria-hidden="true">
      {TRIBES.map((t) => (
        <span data-c={t.color} style={`background-image:url(/mtg/img/${t.key}.jpg)`}></span>
      ))}
    </div>
    <span class="pips">
      {['W', 'U', 'B', 'R', 'G'].map((c) => <span class={`pip pip-${c}`}>{c}</span>)}
    </span>
    <p class="eyebrow">Four-player EDH</p>
    <h1>Goonmander</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Setup for xmage, private server, deckbuilder, gooning.
    </p>

    <div class="hero-actions" style="margin-bottom:1.4rem">
      <span class="status" id="server-status" data-state="unknown">
        <span class="dot" id="status-dot"></span>
        <span><strong id="status-text">Checking…</strong> <small id="status-sub"></small></span>
      </span>
    </div>

    <div class="connect" style="margin-bottom:1.4rem">
      <span class="field">
        <span class="k">Server</span><code>{HOST}</code>
        <button class="ghost tiny" data-copy={HOST}>Copy</button>
      </span>
      <span class="field">
        <span class="k">Port</span><code>{PORT}</code>
        <button class="ghost tiny" data-copy={PORT}>Copy</button>
      </span>
    </div>

    <div class="hero-actions">
      <a class="btn" href="#start">How to join</a>
      <a class="btn ghost" href="/mtg/decks/">Browse all decks</a>
      {DISCORD && <a class="btn ghost" href={DISCORD} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Discord</a>}
    </div>
  </section>


  <section class="section" id="featured">
    <div class="section-head">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Ready to play</p>
        <h2>What people are playing</h2>
        <p>The most-viewed decks right now, split by power bracket. Open one to see the list, change it, and export.</p>
      </div>
      <a class="btn ghost" href="/mtg/decks/">See all <span id="all-count"></span> →</a>
    </div>
    <div id="featured-decks"></div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="start">
    <div class="section-head">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">First time?</p>
        <h2>Getting in</h2>
      </div>
    </div>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li class="step">
        <h3>Install XMage</h3>
        <p>
          A free program that plays Magic with every rule enforced, so nobody has
          to remember how the stack works. It is a desktop app, not a website.
        </p>
        <p><a class="btn" href="https://xmage.today/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Download XMage</a></p>
        <ul>
          <li>Take the <strong>full</strong> download, about 250&nbsp;MB.</li>
          <li>Unzip anywhere, then run <code>run-LAUNCHER.cmd</code> on Windows, or the <code>XMageLauncher</code> jar on Mac and Linux.</li>
          <li>The launcher handles Java and updates itself. Say yes to what it offers.</li>
        </ul>
      </li>
      <li class="step">
        <h3>Connect</h3>
        <p>
          Hit <strong>Launch Client</strong>, choose <strong>Connect to another
          server</strong>, and enter <code>{HOST}</code> on port <code>{PORT}</code>.
          Pick any username and leave the password blank — there is no registration.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li class="step">
        <h3>Bring a deck</h3>
        <p>
          Grab one from above, or paste a list you already have into the converter
          below. Drop the <code>.dck</code> file into the <code>Decks</code> folder
          inside your XMage directory.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li class="step">
        <h3>Sit down</h3>
        <p>
          Open <strong>Tables</strong>, create a game, pick <strong>Commander</strong>
          and four players. Put a password on the table if you want a private pod.
          Voice on Discord, or use the in-client chat.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="convert">
    <div class="section-head">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Already have a list?</p>
        <h2>Convert a decklist</h2>
        <p>
          Paste from Moxfield, Archidekt, or plain text. Every card gets checked
          against what XMage actually refuses, before you download.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <form id="convert-form">
      <input type="text" id="deckname" placeholder="Deck name" style="width:100%;max-width:22rem;margin-bottom:0.6rem" />
      <textarea
        id="decklist"
        aria-label="Decklist"
        placeholder={"1 Atraxa, Praetors' Voice\n1 Sol Ring\n1 Arcane Signet\n…\n\nA Commander section or Moxfield *CMDR* markers are understood."}
      ></textarea>
      <div class="hero-actions" style="margin-top:0.6rem">
        <button type="submit">Check and convert</button>
        <button type="button" class="ghost" id="convert-clear">Clear</button>
      </div>
    </form>
    <div id="convert-out"></div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="section-head"><div><h2>Good to know</h2></div></div>

    <details class="faq">
      <summary>What do the bracket numbers mean?</summary>
      <p>
        Wizards' own power scale. Bracket 2 is precon level, 3 is an upgraded
        deck, 4 is seriously tuned. The number shown is worked out from the
        deck's actual contents — how many cards from the official Game Changers
        list it runs, plus whether it does mass land destruction or chains extra
        turns. Agree a bracket before you shuffle and the evening goes better.
      </p>
    </details>

    <details class="faq">
      <summary>Do I need to buy anything?</summary>
      <p>No. XMage gives you every card ever printed. That is rather the point.</p>
    </details>

    <details class="faq">
      <summary>The table says it is down.</summary>
      <p>
        The server runs around the clock, so this should be rare. The check runs
        every few minutes and can lag, so try connecting anyway before assuming
        the worst — then shout on Discord.
      </p>
    </details>

    <details class="faq">
      <summary>XMage rejected my deck.</summary>
      <p>
        Nearly always one of three things: not exactly 100 cards, a card outside
        your commander's colour identity, or something on this server's ban list
        (stricter than paper — Mana Crypt, Jeweled Lotus, Dockside and Nadu are
        all out). The converter above tells you which before you load the client.
      </p>
    </details>

    <details class="faq">
      <summary>Where do deck files go?</summary>
      <p>
        The <code>Decks</code> folder inside wherever you unzipped XMage. Drop the
        <code>.dck</code> in and it appears when you sit down at a table.
      </p>
    </details>
  </section>

  <p class="credits" style="margin-top:2.5rem">
    Header art:
    {credits.map((c, i) => (
      <span>{i > 0 ? ' · ' : ''}<a href={c.scryfall} target="_blank" rel="noopener">{c.card}</a> by {c.artist}</span>
    ))}
  </p>

  <script>
    import '../../client/home.js';
  </script>
</Pod>
